Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 17:01:54 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 57/71] Condense exec output before it is truncated rather than
 after

`shrink_exec_output` ran in `to_response_item`, on text that
`truncated_output_with_policy` had already cut to the model's budget and that
`response_header` had already been prepended to. Both orderings were wrong, and
the recorded sessions show what they cost. One `Get-ChildItem -Recurse`
returned 16,154 tokens of which 82% were paths under `node_modules`; the budget
was spent on those and the middle truncation dropped the project's own files to
make room. And the header is four to five lines, so `KEEP_HEAD_LINES = 5` was
keeping `Chunk ID`, `Wall time`, `Process exited with code 0` and
`Original token count` rather than the first lines of what ran.

Condensing now happens on the raw text, before either step, in three stages
ordered cheapest and safest first. `normalize` is lossless and unconditional:
escape sequences, carriage-return overwrites, `\r\n` terminators and the column
padding `Format-Table` and `Select-String` add carry nothing a reader could act
on. `filter_artifact_paths` drops listing lines under a generated directory,
matching path components rather than the text of a line so an ignore file that
names `dist` keeps its lines. `shrink_text` is unchanged and still last, so it
counts the command's own lines.

Two gates are new. A poll of a still-running session carries no command, so the
allowlist could never match it, and it has no exit code, so the clean-exit gate
refused it -- yet that is where output is largest: one measured `write_stdin`
poll returned 45,319 tokens of server log whose only useful line, the traceback
the model then fixed, was the last one. It is now shrinkable on the strength of
the live process. And a lossy stage must remove more than the notice explaining
it: the stages are gated on line counts, and a `git commit` printing fifty
one-word lines clears every line threshold while amounting to less than the
notice.

The notice itself is only written for the lossy stages. Announcing the lossless
ones would have cost more than they save -- 59% of recorded outputs carry some
padding or a stray escape sequence, against 12-25% that lose content -- so the
header names what the model can no longer see and nothing else, and the marker
in the body only marks where the gap is, since the header carries the rest one
screen above.

Measured by replaying the real condenser over 892 recorded tool outputs:
476,400 tokens to 439,130, a 7.8% reduction, with 389 tokens spent on nine
notices and no output where a notice cost more than it saved. That replay also
caught a bug this change would otherwise have shipped: taking the last
carriage-return-separated segment of a line deletes every line of Windows
output, because a trailing `\r` is the terminator rather than the start of an
overwrite. It read as a 75.9% saving.

Not validated live.

